# Typed Signals

## Overview

`Jido.SignalAction` defines a Signal input contract and an Action in one
module. It can also define an output Signal contract for correlated replies.

| Capability | API |
| --- | --- |
| Input Signal | `type/0`, `new/2`, `new!/2`, `validate_data/1` |
| Action | `run/2` |
| Output Signal | `reply/3` |
| Route | `route MySignalAction` |

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Define a Direct SignalAction

```elixir
defmodule Typed.AddItem do
  use Jido.SignalAction,
    name: "add_item",
    description: "Adds one item to the cart",
    vsn: 1,
    input: [
      type: "cart.item.add",
      default_source: "/guides/typed",
      schema:
        Zoi.object(%{
          sku: Zoi.string(),
          quantity: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(1) |> Zoi.default(1)
        })
    ]

  @impl true
  def run(%{sku: sku, quantity: quantity}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | items: Map.update(state.items, sku, quantity, &(&1 + quantity))}}
  end
end
```

`new/2` validates input data and builds a Signal. It runs no Action:

```elixir
{:ok, signal} = Typed.AddItem.new(%{sku: "tea"})
{signal.type, signal.source, signal.data}
```

The result is
`{"cart.item.add", "/guides/typed", %{quantity: 1, sku: "tea"}}`.

## Route by Module

```elixir
defmodule Typed.Cart do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "cart"

  agent do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        items: Zoi.map(Zoi.string(), Zoi.integer()) |> Zoi.default(%{}),
        quotes: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)
      })
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/typed")
    route Typed.AddItem, as: :add_item
  end
end
```

The route path comes from `Typed.AddItem.type/0`. The SignalAction is also the
handler. A direct Agent route must return the complete next Agent state.

```elixir
agent = Typed.Cart.instantiate!(id: "cart-1")
{:ok, candidate, []} = Typed.Cart.cmd(agent, signal)
candidate.state
```

The result is `%{items: %{"tea" => 1}, quotes: 0}`.

## Define a Reply Contract

Use `output:` when a SignalAction owns the output Signal shape:

```elixir
defmodule Typed.PriceQuote do
  use Jido.SignalAction,
    name: "price_quote",
    description: "Prices the cart",
    vsn: 1,
    input: [
      type: "cart.quote.request",
      default_source: "/guides/typed",
      schema: Zoi.object(%{items: Zoi.map(Zoi.string(), Zoi.integer())})
    ],
    output: [
      type: "cart.quote.ready",
      default_source: "/guides/typed",
      schema: Zoi.object(%{total_cents: Zoi.integer()})
    ]

  @impl true
  def run(%{items: items}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total_cents: items |> Map.values() |> Enum.sum() |> Kernel.*(450)}}
  end
end
```

`reply/3` owns output Signal construction and validation:

```elixir
request = Typed.PriceQuote.new!(%{items: %{"tea" => 3}})
{:ok, result} = Jido.Exec.run(Typed.PriceQuote, request.data)
{:ok, response} = Typed.PriceQuote.reply(request, result)

{response.type, response.data,
 response.extensions["jidocausationid"] == request.id}
```

The result is
`{"cart.quote.ready", %{total_cents: 1350}, true}`. The response has a new
ID, keeps the request trace, and uses the request ID as its causation ID.

## Use a Different Turn Handler

A SignalAction with an output contract normally computes reply data, not an
Agent state. Use the two-module route form when another Action handles the
Agent Turn:

```elixir
defmodule Typed.AcceptQuote do
  use Jido.Action, name: "accept_quote", schema: Zoi.object(%{items: Zoi.any()})

  @impl true
  def run(_params, %{agent_state: state, signal: request}) do
    with {:ok, result} <- Jido.Exec.run(Typed.PriceQuote, request.data),
         {:ok, response} <- Typed.PriceQuote.reply(request, result) do
      {:ok, %{state | quotes: state.quotes + 1},
       [Jido.Agent.Directive.reply(response)]}
    end
  end
end

defmodule Typed.QuotingCart do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "quoting_cart"

  agent do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        items: Zoi.map(Zoi.string(), Zoi.integer()) |> Zoi.default(%{}),
        quotes: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)
      })
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/typed")
    route Typed.AddItem, as: :add_item
    route Typed.PriceQuote, Typed.AcceptQuote, as: :request_quote
  end
end
```

The path still comes from `Typed.PriceQuote.type/0`, but the Turn runs
`Typed.AcceptQuote`. The SignalAction is not run automatically.

## Reply Rules

`Jido.Agent.Directive.reply/1` is separate from `emit/1`:

- a Reply is one response to the current requester;
- `Typed.PriceQuote.reply/3` builds and validates the response Signal;
- the response causation ID must equal the current request ID;
- one Turn can return at most one Reply;
- `cmd/3` returns the Reply as data and sends nothing; and
- a live Agent Server sends the Reply only after commit.

## Run It Live

```elixir
quoting =
  Typed.QuotingCart.instantiate!(
    id: "cart-2",
    state: %{items: %{"tea" => 3}}
  )

{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, cart} = Jido.start_agent(quoting)
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(cart)

{:ok, committed, [_reply]} = Jido.AgentServer.call(cart, request)

response =
  receive do
    %Jido.Signal{} = response -> {response.type, response.data}
  after
    1_000 -> :no_reply
  end

{committed.state.quotes, response}
```

The state commits before the response Signal is sent.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(cart)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- `route MySignalAction` uses the SignalAction as the handler.
- `route MySignalAction, MyTurn` uses its Signal type and runs `MyTurn`.
- `SignalAction.reply/3` owns response construction, validation, trace, and
  causation.
- `Jido.Agent.Directive.reply/1` requests one post-commit response.

